Matrix addition and subtraction are defined only if the two matrices are the same size, in which case corresponding entries are added or subtracted.\begin{pmatrix}
	a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3 \\ \vdots \\ a_N
\end{pmatrix}
+\begin{pmatrix}
	b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \\ \vdots \\ b_N
\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}
	a_1+b_1 \\ a_2+b_2 \\ a_3+b_3 \\ \vdots \\ a_N+b_N
\end{pmatrix}

Matrix addition and subtraction are distributive:\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{B} + \mathbf{A} \\
\mathbf{A} - \mathbf{B} = -(\mathbf{B} - \mathbf{A})

Multiplying (or dividing) a matrix by a 

scalar means multiplying (or dividing) each entry by that scalar, e.g.:c \mathbf{P} = \begin{pmatrix}
	c p_1 \\ c p_2 \\ c p_3 \\ \vdots \\ c p_N
\end{pmatrix}

where c is a scalar.

Suppose we want to calculate \mathbf{Q}, the product of the matrix \mathbf{M} and the vector \mathbf{P},\mathbf{Q} = \mathbf{M} \mathbf{P}.

where \mathbf{M} is N \times N and \mathbf{P} is N \times 1.

The ith row of \mathbf{M} is\begin{pmatrix}
m_{i,1} &  m_{i,2} & m_{i,3} & \dots & m_{i,N-1} & m_{i,N}
\end{pmatrix}

To obtain the i^{th} entry in \mathbf{Q}, the transpose of the i^{th} row of \mathbf{M} is set alongside of \mathbf{P}, multiplied term by term, and summed:q_i = m_{i,1} p_1 + m_{i,2} p_2 + m_{i,3} p_3 + \dots + m_{i,N-1} p_{N-1} + m_{i,N} p_N

Repeating this for each row in  \mathbf{M} results in a vector \mathbf{Q} that is the same size as \mathbf{P}.

Note that this is possible only if the number of columns in \mathbf{M} is equal to the number of rows in \mathbf{P}. Matrix multiplication is defined only if this condition is met.

For example, the multiplication \mathbf{P} \mathbf{M} is not defined, because each row in \mathbf{P} has only one column.
That means the term-by-term multiplication operation cannot be executed.

In the default settings (to reproduce these, just restart the kernel in the menu at the top left):

the seed source is set to h = 10 m;

the seed’s sinking speed is U = 0.5 \frac{m}{s};

the rate at which seeds are released is one per second: n = 1 s^{-1};

the default wind speed is W = 1 \frac{m}{s}; and,

the default turbulent mixing intensity is K = 0.5 \frac{m^2}{s}.

You can modify these as needed to apply to a specific problem, by entering a new value in a textbox and hitting enter.

